{"step_index":1258,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-28T23:48:11Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nThe counter I was reaching for is store-to-load forwarding stalls, the PMU event that fires when a core writes a value and immediately reads it back from the same address, forcing a pipeline bubble. That's the one that will tell you whether your 1.43 ns is genuine memory traffic or the CPU shortcutting through its own forwarding logic. Pair it with L1D miss latency and you've got the full picture: stores retired, stalls taken, misses incurred. If the stalls are near zero and the misses are near zero at 5.85 cycles, the M4 is doing something its documentation doesn't describe, and that's a finding, not a measurement error. Lets find the true depth of how we make it do more.  /goal \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-28T19:48:11-04:00.\n\nThe user has mentioned some items in the form @[ITEM]. Here is extra information about the items that were mentioned by the user, in the order that they appear:\n\n/goal is a [Slash Command]:\nThe user has marked this task with /goal, indicating that this task is intended to run for a long time without user input, e.g. overnight. You should be extra thorough and only stop when you are confident the goal has been completely fulfilled. The system will force you to continue execution, prompting you to audit your work until completion. Once complete, include <!-- GOAL_COMPLETE --> in your response. If the user explicitly asked to stop or cancel this goal, include <!-- GOAL_CANCELLED --> in your response to cancel the goal.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":1259,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-28T23:48:11Z","thinking":"**Defining The Counter**\n\nI've homed in on store-to-load forwarding stalls. That's the PMU event I'm after, the one that flags when a core writes and immediately reads a value from the same address, causing a pipeline bubble. Identifying this specific event will be the key to understanding the performance impact. This focus should help reach the 1.43 goal.\n\n\n**Analyzing Memory Traffic**\n\nI am now focused on distinguishing genuine memory traffic from CPU forwarding. I'll pair this with L1D miss latency. When stalls and misses are near zero at 5.85 cycles, the M4 is behaving in a way its documentation doesn't explain. This is a crucial finding that needs further investigation to uncover the true capabilities of the M4 and Sovra compiler.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"bash /Users/ajoxendine68/Desktop/SSOT_Agentic_Brain/ops/scripts/agent_speak.sh jarvis \"Goal accepted Anthony. Initiating deep hardware microarchitecture analysis of M4 Store-to-Load forwarding, decoupled store-readback pipelines, and SIMD ARM64 compiler lowerings.\"","Cwd":"/Volumes/Untitled/SSOT_Agentic_Brain_v1.1.0_ENGINEERING_RELEASE","IsDaemon":false,"RequestedTerminalID":"","RunPersistent":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":10000,"toolAction":"Jarvis announcing goal initiation","toolSummary":"Jarvis announcing goal initiation"}}]}
